{
  "title": "Fourth and fifth declensions",
  "note": "Fourth-declension nouns have genitive -us: manus, manus (f), hand. Most are masculine, but manus and domus are feminine, and cornu and genu are neuter. Fifth-declension nouns end in -es, genitive -ei: res, rei (f), thing. They are feminine except dies, day, usually masculine. Res is everywhere: res publica, the state.",
  "src": {
    "t": "Allen and Greenough, 4th Declension",
    "u": "https://dcc.dickinson.edu/grammar/latin/4th-declension-stem-paradigm-and-gender"
  },
